// runnable entry on its own, runs its tests, and puts the file back.
//
// A runnable entry: {id, step, name, file, from, to, tests, also?, equivalent?, note?}
//   - `from` is copied exactly from the current file and occurs there exactly once; `to` is the faulty replacement.
//   - `also` lists further edits of the same fault ({file?, from, to}), applied together with the first.
//   - `equivalent` says why surviving is expected (the guard is repeated elsewhere by design, or the change is timing
//     only); such an entry is still run and reported on its own line.
//   - `tests` are the existing test files most likely to catch it, or files being written now (NEW_TEST_FILES).
// An owed entry: {id, step, name, owed}: the fault guards code that does not exist yet, and `owed` says where and when
// it becomes testable.
// `name` is the spec bullet text when the entry comes from section 7, otherwise the fault as its source names it.
// Data only: nothing here reads or changes a file.

